 | Workgroup barriers | `s_barrier` (cs/ms) is one `OpControlBarrier` (workgroup scope, workgroup memory). On RDNA every `s_barrier` counts against the same workgroup barrier and a wave that has ended no longer counts, while Vulkan requires a barrier to be reached by every invocation in uniform control flow. Under `--wave64-emulate` the round loop keeps a single barrier at the top level, so this holds. **Native wave32 compute shaders run in barrier rounds** when an `s_barrier` sits anywhere but a top-level block of the structured tree (one every invocation runs): under a guard, in a loop, or in the dispatch loop (`needs_barrier_rounds`, `bar_scheduler`). That is the emulated loop without its cross-lane part: `s_barrier` ends a segment, each invocation runs its blocks until its next `s_barrier` or the end, then the whole workgroup meets at one `OpControlBarrier` (the same workgroup/acquire-release/workgroup-memory semantics) at the end of the round. A wave that is done no longer holds the others, and a per-round count of the invocations not done (three `Workgroup` words, by round mod 3) ends the loop. A wave that is done no longer holds the others, and a per-round count of the invocations not done (three `Workgroup` words, by round mod 3, or a borrowed LDS word with a large LDS: "Barrier-round count in LDS (SHADER-579)") ends the loop. Cross-lane operations stay subgroup operations, as in the dispatch loop, since a wave's lanes run the same segment. A barrier in a top-level block keeps the single `OpControlBarrier` in place. In the 1.0.2.1 corpus 43 of the 55 wave32 cs shaders with `s_barrier` have one nested (SHADER-91's count), so those 43 now run in rounds. Why: an Intel GPU does not survive the nested form. `cs_barrier_divergent.asm` (two barrier sites in different branches), `cs_barrier_early_end.asm` (the other waves end without a barrier) and `cs_barrier_loop.asm` (a barrier in a loop whose trip count differs per wave) each lose the device (`vkQueueWaitIdle` →