 | Barrier-round count in LDS (SHADER-579) | Barrier rounds (see "Workgroup barriers") count the invocations not done in `%x_buf`, three `Workgroup` dwords (#930). On a full 32 KiB LDS that made 32,780 B, 12 bytes over the 32,768 B `maxComputeSharedMemorySize` of the AMD Windows (RX 9070 XT) and Intel (UHD 770) drivers, so the runtime refused those pipelines (2 native wave32 cs in towade's 1.0.2.1 corpus, [finding](https://github.com/ItsNotRexed/Leonida/pull/1882#issuecomment-7084298032)). Rule (`bar_counts_in_lds`): when the shader uses its LDS and the LDS array plus 12 bytes is over 16,384 B (the Vulkan minimum limit, so in practice an array of 16, 32 or 64 KiB), there is no `%x_buf`. The count borrows LDS word 0 at the end of each round (`bar_scheduler_tail`): after the round's barrier every invocation is done or waits at `s_barrier`, so no shader code runs; invocation 0 saves the word in a Function variable and clears it, barrier, every invocation not done adds 1 atomically, barrier, all read the count, barrier, invocation 0 stores the saved word back, barrier. The shader sees its LDS bit for bit as before, and the four extra barriers sit where the whole workgroup already waits, so `s_barrier`'s semantics are unchanged; the cost is four more `OpControlBarrier`s per round in those shaders only. Below the threshold `%x_buf` stays (12 bytes fit every device) with one barrier per round; a shader that never touches its LDS also keeps it (no array to borrow from, 12 B in all). A 32 KiB LDS is now exactly 32,768 B and a 64 KiB one 65,536 B (before: 65,548 B, refused or shrunk to its allocation). Not chosen: putting the count in LDS the shader never touches (unprovable with dynamic addresses), or deciding per device (the translator runs offline). Pinned by `tests/semantic/test_cs_barrier_counts_lds.py` (Workgroup bytes from the emitted SPIR-V and `lds_bytes` for 32 KiB, 64 KiB and small LDS, under a 32 KiB `--max-shared-memory`; ring shifts across 10 barriers that read word 0 and an early-ending wave in the workgroup interpreter, whose LDS starts as 0xDEADBEEF, in both wave orders). Confirmed by those CPU tests only; GPU runs on the 32 KiB devices are pending. |
